<CachingArticle slug="keys-and-values">
 <p>Two visitors open the blue-mug page. One expects a price in euros, the other in dollars. The application asks Redis for <code>product:blue-mug</code> both times.</p>
 <p>Redis can answer the second request perfectly and still give the visitor the wrong price. It has been asked the same question twice. The application meant to ask two different questions.</p>
 <p>In <a href="/learn/databases/caching/">the introduction</a>, a saved answer let later requests skip the catalogue. We now need to decide which requests are allowed to use that answer. A key is the application's claim that those requests are interchangeable.</p>
 <p>Our shop has grown into a service hosting two shops, north and south. The north shop also offers a trade price. The four cards below ask for the same mug identifier but expect different answers. Add fields to the key and watch the requests separate into groups.</p>
 <KeysAndValues kind="identity" />
 <p>With only the product in the key, A saves the north shop's retail euro price. Every later request finds that answer. Seven of eight requests are hits, but six receive an answer that belongs to a different question. Including shop, currency and price group gives four saved answers. The second round still reuses all four, and every response now belongs to its request.</p>

 <section>
  <h2>The boundary around an answer</h2>
  <p>A useful rule is: if two requests produce the same key, the saved value must be safe to use for either request, within the freshness promise we have chosen. We don't need every property of the request in the key. We need every property that changes the meaning or permitted audience of the cached answer.</p>
  <p>For our product response, that may include the shop, product, currency, price group and language. The visitor's screen width probably doesn't change the JSON response, so including it would split one reusable answer into unnecessary copies. A request identifier would be worse: making each key unique would eliminate reuse entirely.</p>
  <p>Now suppose a trade customer has a negotiated price that differs from every other customer's. A broad <code>trade</code> key no longer identifies the answer. We might include the customer account, or cache the common description separately and fetch the negotiated price. There is no key convention that creates sharing where the underlying answers differ.</p>
  <p>The same reasoning applies to permissions. A cached document may be visible to one account and private to another. A fast hit must not bypass the application's access check. Establish the caller's authorised shop and account scope before looking up a scoped answer. Derive that scope from trusted application context rather than accepting an arbitrary shop identifier from a request header.</p>
  <p>Putting a shop name in the key prevents accidental name collisions when the application constructs keys correctly. It is not an access-control boundary enforced automatically by Redis. Another application with permission to read those keys can still request them. We will separate Redis credentials and network access from application authorisation in the operations article.</p>
  <p>Each added input creates more possible entries. Ten products, two currencies, three price groups and two languages permit 120 combinations. We don't have to populate them all, but that is the space our workload can explore. This is why correctness and reuse need to be considered together: first identify answers that may safely be shared, then measure whether requests actually return to those groups.</p>
 </section>

 <section>
  <h2>Giving the question a stable name</h2>
  <p>Redis keys are byte sequences. Colons are a naming convention, not a folder hierarchy or a schema that Redis understands. A name such as <code>product:north:blue-mug:EUR</code> is convenient for people to read, but the application must still build it unambiguously.</p>
  <p>Consider joining a shop identifier and product identifier with a colon. The pairs <code>["a:b", "c"]</code> and <code>["a", "b:c"]</code> both become <code>a:b:c</code>. If those identifiers are allowed, two different questions collapse into one key.</p>
  <p>One solution is to encode a fixed sequence of typed fields rather than joining raw values. Here is a JavaScript key for our product response. Its inputs have already been validated and converted to the application's agreed forms:</p>
  <pre><code>{String.raw`function productResponseKey(context, product) {
  return JSON.stringify([
    "product-response", 2,
    context.shopId,
    product.id,
    context.currency,
    context.priceGroup,
    context.locale
  ]);
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>The result might be <code>["product-response",2,"north","blue-mug","EUR","retail","en-GB"]</code>. The quotes, commas and escaping preserve the field boundaries. The leading name separates this purpose from other uses of Redis, and the number identifies the value format. If several languages produce keys, specify the exact encoding and keep shared test cases: two different encodings of the same logical tuple would cause avoidable misses.</p>
  <p>Stable naming also requires stable interpretation of inputs. If <code>currency=eur</code> and <code>currency=EUR</code> mean the same thing in our API, validate and normalise them to one supported currency code before forming the key. If product identifiers are case-sensitive, changing their case would instead merge distinct products. Normalisation has to follow the application's meaning.</p>
  <p>For a filtered search, parameter order may not matter, but list order might. Sorting a set of selected categories can be appropriate; sorting a ranked list of preferred suppliers can change the question. Likewise, an omitted limit and an explicit default limit may be equivalent only after the API's default has been applied. Construct a canonical request description from parsed inputs, not from an arbitrary raw query string.</p>
  <p>Long descriptions can be hashed to a fixed-length suffix after canonicalisation. Hashing does not repair omitted inputs, and it does not make a key an authorisation check. Keep credentials and unnecessary personal data out of key names; names often appear in operational tooling. A short purpose prefix plus a well-defined encoding is usually easier to operate than either an opaque name everywhere or a copy of the entire HTTP request.</p>
 </section>

 <section>
  <h2>Bytes need an agreement</h2>
  <p>The key identifies the question. The value needs a contract too. Suppose the old application stores <code>18</code> to mean eighteen euros. A new release switches to integer cents and stores <code>1800</code>. Both are valid Redis strings and valid JSON numbers.</p>
  <p>During a gradual deployment, old and new application instances may run together. Choose different reader and writer formats below. Then include the format version in the key.</p>
  <KeysAndValues kind="schema" />
  <p>Without a format boundary, the old reader displays eighteen hundred euros, while a new reader encountering the old value displays eighteen cents. Neither case requires a Redis failure. The clients disagree about the meaning of successful data.</p>
  <p>Separate <code>v1</code> and <code>v2</code> keys let each release use the format it understands. The first request for the new format misses and builds its own value. That costs extra work during rollout, and the two versions may temporarily consume more memory. It also makes rollback possible without forcing the old reader to decode the new format.</p>
  <p>A version number in the value can serve a related purpose: the reader can reject an unsupported representation instead of silently interpreting it. Validate the decoded structure, required fields and units. A cache miss, a malformed value and a network failure should remain distinguishable in measurements even if the application chooses a source read for more than one of them.</p>
  <p>A format version is not a data revision. <code>v2</code> says how to interpret the bytes; it doesn't say whether the mug's price has changed since they were saved. Versioned data and versioned formats solve different problems. We will need the former when we examine concurrent fills.</p>
  <p>For this collection, monetary examples that use cents carry an explicit currency and integer amount. In a real application, specify each currency's unit and rounding rules rather than assuming every currency has two decimal places. Dates, time zones, large integers, absent fields and explicit nulls need equally deliberate representation choices.</p>
 </section>

 <section>
  <h2>One value or several fields?</h2>
  <p>A string containing a complete JSON response is a useful starting point. One <code>GET</code> retrieves it; one <code>SET</code> replaces it. The reader gets a complete stored value. If the application always needs the whole response, storing it as one value keeps the cache conversation small.</p>
  <p>Redis also has a <strong>hash</strong> type: one key holding a mapping of field names to values. We could store <code>name</code>, <code>currency</code> and <code>amount</code> as separate fields. <code>HSET</code> writes specified fields, and <code>HMGET</code> retrieves a chosen list. A hash lets us work on a small part without transferring a large complete response.</p>
  <p>Splitting fields introduces a question about which changes belong together. Suppose we are replacing an offer of EUR 18.00 with USD 20.00. In the next illustration, pause after the first of two writes. Then select a single write for the pair and repeat.</p>
  <KeysAndValues kind="fields" />
  <p>Between two separate <code>HSET</code> commands, the hash contains the new currency and the old amount. A reader can accurately retrieve a pair that the application never intended to publish. One <code>HSET</code> containing both fields changes them as one command, so another client cannot read halfway through that command. Replacing one complete JSON string has the same useful boundary for the whole value.</p>
  <p>The read needs a boundary as well. Reading currency with one command and amount with another allows a writer to change the pair between those reads. Use one <code>HMGET</code> for the related fields. This gives a view of the fields at one command boundary on that server. It doesn't prove they were derived from a fresh, consistent source read, or that a later failover will retain them.</p>
  <p>Hashes also update rather than replace their entire mapping. If a new response omits an obsolete field, simply writing the remaining fields doesn't remove the old one. Define how fields are deleted, or publish a new complete representation under a new format key. Otherwise old data can survive a schema change even though every write succeeds.</p>
  <p>The right size of a value follows the application's work. A shared description and a private price may deserve separate cache entries because their audiences and change rates differ. Fields that must describe one coherent offer may belong in a single value. Splitting a response can save repeated bytes, but it adds lookups and allows pieces to have different ages. Measure the complete request path, including decoding and allocation, rather than choosing a data type solely from its command speed.</p>
 </section>

 <section>
  <h2>An answer can be “not found”</h2>
  <p>A visitor follows an old link to a product that no longer exists. The cache is empty, so the application asks the catalogue. The catalogue successfully reports that there is no such product. If we save nothing, the next visitor repeats the same search.</p>
  <p>We can cache the result of that search too. Select <strong>Save a confirmed “not found” answer</strong> below, then change the source outcome to a timeout.</p>
  <KeysAndValues kind="negative" />
  <p>Saving a confirmed absence is called <strong>negative caching</strong>. The value <code>{'{"found":false}'}</code> is a real entry. A missing Redis key means “we haven't got an answer”; this value means “we looked and found no product”. The application must not confuse the two.</p>
  <p>A source timeout says something different: we don't know whether the product exists. Turning that failure into a cached absence would make a temporary outage look like a successful “not found” response. Keep errors as errors and apply an explicit failure policy. The illustration deliberately refuses to save an absence when the source failed.</p>
  <p>A negative answer can become stale just like a price. If the shop creates the product after we cache its absence, the saved “not found” can hide it until expiry or invalidation. Choose its lifetime from the cost of that delay and the cost of repeated source lookups. Apply the same shop, visibility and representation rules as for positive answers.</p>
  <p>An explicit envelope also avoids treating valid values such as zero, an empty list or an empty string as misses. At the cache boundary, distinguish the client's missing-key result from a successfully decoded value. A truthiness test is usually too vague for that job.</p>
 </section>

 <section>
  <h2>Try the representation boundaries</h2>
  <p>Start a fresh disposable Redis 8.2.2 container. As in the introduction, it publishes no port and retains no data after it is stopped:</p>
  <pre><code>{String.raw`docker run --rm -d --name caching-keys redis:8.2.2 \
  redis-server --save "" --appendonly no
docker exec -it caching-keys redis-cli`}</code></pre>
  <p>First store a hash, then read the price pair. <code>HSET</code> returns the number of newly added fields, not the number whose values changed:</p>
  <pre><code>{String.raw`> HSET offer currency EUR amount 1800
(integer) 2
> HMGET offer currency amount
1) "EUR"
2) "1800"
> HSET offer currency USD
(integer) 0
> HMGET offer currency amount
1) "USD"
2) "1800"
> HSET offer currency USD amount 2000
(integer) 0
> HMGET offer currency amount
1) "USD"
2) "2000"`}</code></pre>
  <p>The middle read reproduces the mixed pair. The final command writes both fields together. Asking <code>GET offer</code> now produces a wrong-type error, because <code>GET</code> reads a string rather than a hash. Commands operate on specific Redis types; JSON inside a string is still a Redis string.</p>
  <p>Now give the hash a lifetime with <code>EXPIRE offer 60</code>, change its amount with <code>HSET offer amount 2100</code>, and inspect <code>TTL offer</code>. Changing a field preserves the key's existing expiry. By contrast, an ordinary <code>SET</code> replacing a string discards its old TTL unless you supply an expiry or explicitly preserve it. Keeping expiry attached to every fill is part of the application's write contract. The separate hash commands here are an observation exercise; a failure between initial creation and <code>EXPIRE</code> would leave a key without that intended deadline.</p>
  <p>Finally, store <code>SET missing-product '{'{"found":false}'}' EX 5</code> and read it with <code>GET missing-product</code>. For a few seconds you receive the negative answer; afterwards the Redis lookup itself misses. Leave the client with <code>QUIT</code> and run <code>docker stop caching-keys</code>.</p>
  <p>The commands and their boundary cases were checked against Redis 8.2.2. The source lookups and deployment choices in the illustrations are application models, not Redis features that construct keys or validate values for us.</p>
 </section>

 <section>
  <h2>The promise attached to a key</h2>
  <p>We now have a more precise description of an entry. It names an authorised, normalised question, carries a representation the reader understands, and groups the pieces that must be read and written together. Even “not found” has an explicit meaning.</p>
  <p>These decisions are worth testing with pairs of requests. Change each input that should affect the answer and check that its key changes. Change an irrelevant input and check that it doesn't. Try delimiter characters, omitted defaults, different authorisation scopes and readers from two application releases. A few carefully chosen pairs reveal more than checking that one ordinary key looks tidy.</p>
  <p>One problem remains even when every one of those tests passes. The answer can belong to exactly the right question and still be too old. To understand that, we need to follow a copy through time—and pay attention to the interval between reading the source and saving the result.</p>
 </section>
